{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>The waters piled up.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): as in its Targum, חכימו מיא — 'the waters grew wise.' The intention is as though the waters played the wise one when they stood up as a single heap to provide a path through the sea for the redeemed to pass through, and to shake out the Egyptians into it afterward, when the waters would return as at first to their accustomed flow." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'נערמו מים' — the waters grew wise — is as in (Numbers 4:13) 'ודשנו את המזבח,' and it would be the opposite of התחכמות, as if to say: wisdom was lost from the waters. And the explanation of the matter is that the flow from the attribute of Chesed ceased — it no longer received from above — and therefore it does not say 'נערמו מי הים' but 'נערמו מים,' so that you should understand it according to its plain sense — actual waters." "<b>And you should understand</b> further in the expression 'waters' (מים), that it alludes to the attribute of Chesed. And when the flow from the attribute of Chesed ceased and did not extend below to the separate intelligences in the maintenance of the world according to its custom and nature, therefore the sea was split and all the waters of the world were split, for the prince of the sea is among the separate forces. And a proof for my words is what I found in the Targum Yerushalmi: 'קפאו תהומות בלב ים בגו פלגס דימא רבא' — 'the deeps froze in the heart of the sea, within the channel of the Great Sea.' And this was the cause of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds. And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:31.458185559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}